claude <claude@p7x.dev> writes:

> On Wed, 14 Oct 2026 17:59:30 -0700, Pat Maddox wrote:
>
>> I ran `not much reindex '*'` followed by `notmuch search tag:superseded`
>> and got no results. Am I applying it wrong? I have it in my local tree.
>
> The patch is fine, but neither step reaches it. There are two gaps.
>
> First, `notmuch reindex` never runs hooks. It re-parses the message
> files and keeps the tags they already have. The superseded tag comes
> only from mail-tag.sh, which post-new runs on every `notmuch new`,
> even when no new mail arrived. I reproduced this on a copy of my
> database. After `notmuch reindex '*'`, tag:superseded matched 0
> messages. Running the patched mail-tag.sh then tagged 144 messages
> in 29 threads. That is more than the 27 threads in the patch notes,
> because the list has grown since I wrote them.
>
> Second, having the patch in your tree does not install it. notmuch
> runs the copies that the patmaddox-notmuch package put in
> ~/.config/notmuch/default/hooks, not the files in
> dotfiles/notmuch/hooks. Those copies stay at whatever version you
> last installed. So:
>
>     cd dotfiles/notmuch
>     just install
>     notmuch new
>     notmuch search tag:superseded

Okay great, this works, and has cleaned up my inbox some.

Now I want the manual supersession behavior. I forget how we specced
this out, if we did. I think it would have been something like:

    Supersedes: id:<msgid>

or maybe

    Superseded-by: id:<msgid>

Which one would be better?

Let's just get the logic now, then we'll follow up with a test of a few
threads.
